PAGINE.append(guida("/en/how-much-does-an-app-cost/",
 "How Much Does It Cost to Make an App? 2026 Prices",
 "What an app really costs in 2026: what sets the price, typical US agency ranges vs. our fixed prices, and how to cut cost without cutting quality.",
 "App cost guide",
 "How much does it cost<br>to make <span class=\"grad\">an app in 2026?</span>",
 "Short answer: anywhere from under a thousand to several hundred thousand dollars, which is useless until you know what drives the number. Here are the five factors that decide the price, what typical US quotes look like, and our own published prices.",
 [{"id":"factors","tipo":"cards","eyebrow":"What sets the price",
   "h2":"Five things that<br><span class=\"grad\">decide the number</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Number of distinct screens and flows","Every user journey — sign-up, booking, checkout, admin — is design, code and testing. The count of real flows is the best single predictor of cost."),
    ("Native, cross-platform or web app","Two native apps cost more than one cross-platform app, which costs more than an installable web app. The right choice depends on what the app must do on the phone."),
    ("Integrations","Payments, maps, your CRM, POS or ERP. Each integration is predictable when the other system has a good API, and expensive when it does not."),
    ("Back office","Most apps need an admin panel to manage users, content and orders. It is often a third of the work and often forgotten in early estimates."),
    ("Who builds it, and where","A big-city US agency, a freelancer, an offshore team and a European studio can quote the same app very differently. Rates are driven by overhead and location as much as by skill."),
    ("Maintenance after launch","Operating system updates, store requirements, hosting and small fixes. Budget for it every year.")]},
  {"id":"ranges","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"Typical ranges",
   "h2":"What US quotes<br><span class=\"grad\">usually look like</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Published industry surveys and agency price lists in the US commonly put a simple app in the tens of thousands of dollars, a typical business app with integrations at several tens of thousands, and complex consumer or marketplace apps well into six figures. The spread is wide because \"an app\" covers very different things.",
    "Hourly rates explain much of it: big-city US agencies bill senior developers at rates that reflect big-city salaries and rent. Offshore teams bill far less per hour but often more in coordination time.",
    "Our published prices, for comparison: a ready-made web app from <b>€750</b> one-off or <b>€50 a month</b>; custom software from <b>€1,500</b> for a focused first stage; and every custom app quoted at a fixed price per stage, in writing, after a free call. Most focused business apps land in the low five figures in euros."]},
  {"id":"cut","tipo":"passi","eyebrow":"How to spend less",
   "h2":"Cut the cost,<br>not <span class=\"grad\">the quality</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Start with one flow","Build the single journey that proves the app is useful. Add the rest once real users ask for it."),
    ("Consider a web app","If users are your own staff or customers who log in, an installable web app avoids two native codebases and store reviews."),
    ("Buy what is commodity","Payments, authentication, maps and notifications are services, not things to build from scratch."),
    ("Insist on a fixed price per stage","It forces clear scope and protects you from projects that never end."),
    ("Own the code","Code in your repository and IP assigned to you, so you can change suppliers without starting over.")]}],
 [("How much does a simple app cost?","With us, a ready-made web app starts from €750 and custom work from €1,500 for a first stage. US agency quotes for simple native apps commonly start in the tens of thousands of dollars."),
  ("How long does it take to build an app?","A focused first version typically takes six to ten weeks; larger apps are built in stages over several months."),
  ("Is it cheaper to build a web app than a native app?","Usually, yes: one codebase, no store review, instant updates. Go native when you need store presence or deep device features."),
  ("How much does app maintenance cost per year?","Plan for hosting, store fees and a yearly update budget. Our web apps on the monthly plan include hosting, updates and support.")],
 extra=[("Mobile app development USA","/en/mobile-app-development-company-usa/")]))

PAGINE.append(guida("/en/how-much-does-custom-software-cost/",
 "How Much Does Custom Software Cost? 2026 Guide",
 "What custom business software costs in 2026, what drives the price, buy-vs-build math, and how fixed-price stages keep projects under control. With real prices.",
 "Software cost guide",
 "How much does<br><span class=\"grad\">custom software cost?</span>",
 "Custom software is priced by scope, integrations and risk — not by a list. Here is how to estimate it, how to compare it against a SaaS subscription over three years, and what our own fixed prices look like.",
 [{"id":"drivers","tipo":"cards","eyebrow":"What drives the price",
   "h2":"The cost drivers,<br><span class=\"grad\">in order</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Process complexity","How many roles, steps, exceptions and approvals the software has to model. Messy processes cost more to build — and are exactly where off-the-shelf tools fail."),
    ("Integrations","Accounting, ERP, CRM, e-commerce, email. Good APIs make integrations predictable; legacy systems without them make them expensive."),
    ("Data migration","Moving years of spreadsheets or an old system's data, cleaned and checked. Often underestimated."),
    ("Users and devices","Office only, or field staff on phones, offline, in several languages."),
    ("Security and compliance","Roles, audit logs, encryption, HIPAA-conscious hosting where health data is involved."),
    ("Who builds it","Rates differ greatly between onshore agencies, offshore teams and European studios like ours.")]},
  {"id":"math","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"Buy vs build",
   "h2":"The three-year<br><span class=\"grad\">math</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Compare total cost over three years, not the first invoice. A SaaS tool at a per-seat price, multiplied by users and years, plus the hours your team loses working around the parts that do not fit, is the real number.",
    "If a SaaS product fits ninety percent of your process, buy it — and integrate it. If it fits sixty percent, the missing forty is where the hours go, and custom software built in stages usually pays back within the first year or two.",
    "The middle path is common and often best: keep the platform, build the missing module, interface or integration around it."]},
  {"id":"ours","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"Our prices",
   "h2":"What it costs<br><span class=\"grad\">with us</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Custom business software starts from <b>€1,500</b> for a focused first stage. A typical internal tool or portal lands in the low five figures in euros across its stages; a large ERP-style system more.",
    "Every stage has a fixed price in writing and delivers something usable on its own. You can stop after any stage and keep everything built so far.",
    "Web apps start at <b>€50 a month</b> or <b>€750</b> one-off for ready-made solutions. There is no per-seat license: the code is yours."]}],
 [("How much does custom software cost for a small business?","A focused tool starts from €1,500 with us; most small-business systems land in the low five figures in euros, built in stages."),
  ("Is custom software cheaper than SaaS?","Over three years it often is when per-seat fees are high or the SaaS fits poorly. When a SaaS fits well, buying is cheaper."),
  ("How long does custom software take?","The first usable stage typically goes live in four to eight weeks."),
  ("What are the ongoing costs?","Hosting under your account and optional maintenance. No license fees to us.")],
 extra=[("Software development company USA","/en/software-development-company-usa/")]))

PAGINE.append(guida("/en/ada-website-compliance/",
 "ADA Website Compliance: A Practical 2026 Guide",
 "ADA website compliance explained for US businesses: who gets sued, what WCAG 2.1 AA requires, why overlays don't protect you, and a practical checklist.",
 "ADA compliance",
 "ADA website compliance,<br><span class=\"grad\">without the panic.</span>",
 "Website accessibility lawsuits are filed against US businesses by the thousands every year, and most targets are small and mid-size companies. Here is what the law actually says, what the technical standard requires, and what to do — written by developers, not lawyers, so check the legal side with your counsel.",
 [{"id":"law","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"The legal picture",
   "h2":"What the ADA<br><span class=\"grad\">says about websites</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Title III of the Americans with Disabilities Act requires businesses open to the public to be accessible to people with disabilities. The Department of Justice has stated that this applies to websites, while not writing a specific technical rule for private businesses.",
    "In practice, courts and settlements refer to the <b>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1, level AA</b>. For state and local governments, the Department of Justice adopted WCAG 2.1 AA as the formal standard under Title II in 2024.",
    "Claims are concentrated in a few places — New York and Florida federal courts, and California, where the Unruh Act adds statutory damages — and target retail, restaurants, hospitality, healthcare and professional services above all. Many start with a demand letter rather than a lawsuit."]},
  {"id":"checklist","tipo":"cards","eyebrow":"The technical side",
   "h2":"What WCAG 2.1 AA<br><span class=\"grad\">checks in practice</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Text alternatives","Meaningful alt text for images that carry information; decorative images marked as such. Menus and prices as text, not images."),
    ("Keyboard navigation","Everything usable without a mouse, with a visible focus indicator and a logical order."),
    ("Color contrast","Text contrast of at least 4.5:1 against its background (3:1 for large text)."),
    ("Forms","Every field labeled, errors explained in text, no information conveyed by color alone."),
    ("Structure","Real headings, landmarks and link text that makes sense out of context, so screen readers can navigate."),
    ("Media and motion","Captions on videos, no auto-playing audio, and a way to pause animations.")]},
  {"id":"overlays","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"A warning",
   "h2":"Accessibility overlays<br><span class=\"grad\">are not a fix.</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Widgets that promise one-line ADA compliance are widely criticized by accessibility experts and disability advocates, and sites using them have been sued anyway. In 2025 the Federal Trade Commission took action against one overlay vendor over its compliance claims.",
    "Accessibility is a property of the code: headings, labels, contrast, keyboard behavior. A script layered on top cannot reliably fix markup underneath.",
    "The durable fix is to build or remediate the site to WCAG 2.1 AA and test it with keyboard and screen readers. Every site we build is made that way."]},
  {"id":"steps","tipo":"passi","eyebrow":"What to do",
   "h2":"Five practical<br><span class=\"grad\">steps</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Run an automated scan","Free tools catch roughly a third of issues — contrast, missing alt text, missing labels. A start, not an answer."),
    ("Test by hand","Navigate the key journeys with only a keyboard, then with a screen reader. This is where most real problems show up."),
    ("Fix the templates first","Most issues live in shared templates: fixing the header, menu and product template fixes hundreds of pages."),
    ("Publish an accessibility statement","Say what standard you follow and how to report a problem — and answer when someone does."),
    ("Keep it accessible","New content and features need the same checks. Build it into the publishing process.")]}],
 [("Is my small business website required to be ADA compliant?","If you serve the public, courts have generally applied the ADA to websites, and small businesses are frequent targets. Your counsel can confirm how it applies to you."),
  ("What is the standard for ADA website compliance?","WCAG 2.1 level AA is the standard referenced in most settlements and in the DOJ's 2024 rule for public entities."),
  ("Do accessibility widgets make my site compliant?","No. They do not reliably fix the underlying code, and sites using them have still been sued."),
  ("Can you make our existing site accessible?","Yes. We audit it against WCAG 2.1 AA, fix the templates first, and test with keyboard and screen readers. Sometimes rebuilding is cheaper; we will tell you which.")],
 extra=[("Web design agency USA","/en/web-design-agency-usa/")]))

PAGINE.append(guida("/en/website-privacy-laws-us-states/",
 "Website Privacy Laws by US State: 2026 Guide",
 "Which US privacy laws apply to your website in 2026: CCPA/CPRA, Texas, Colorado and other states, opt-out signals, cookie banners and health data.",
 "Privacy laws",
 "Website privacy laws<br><span class=\"grad\">state by state.</span>",
 "The US has no single federal privacy law for websites; it has a growing patchwork of state laws. Here is a practical overview of what they mean for your site — consent, opt-outs, forms and health data — written by developers. It is not legal advice: confirm your obligations with counsel.",
 [{"id":"map","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"The patchwork",
   "h2":"About twenty states<br>now have <span class=\"grad\">privacy laws.</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "California led with the <b>CCPA</b>, expanded by the <b>CPRA</b>. Since then, roughly twenty states have passed comprehensive consumer privacy laws, including Virginia, Colorado, Connecticut, Utah, Texas, Oregon, Montana, Iowa, Delaware, New Jersey, New Hampshire, Tennessee, Minnesota, Maryland, Indiana, Kentucky and Rhode Island, with effective dates spread from 2023 into 2026.",
    "Most apply only above thresholds — revenue, or the number of residents whose data you process — but the thresholds differ. California's main revenue threshold is around $25 million, adjusted for inflation; Texas applies to most businesses that are not small businesses, and restricts selling sensitive data even for those that are.",
    "Separately, Washington's <b>My Health My Data Act</b> and a similar Nevada law cover consumer health data with no revenue threshold, reaching many fitness, wellness and even some retail sites."]},
  {"id":"site","tipo":"cards","eyebrow":"What it means for a website",
   "h2":"The technical<br><span class=\"grad\">checklist</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Know your trackers","List every pixel, tag and script on the site and what data each sends where. Most sites have more than their owners think."),
    ("Consent that actually works","A banner that blocks non-essential trackers until consent where required — not one that just displays text."),
    ("Opt-out of sale and sharing","Where required, a working \"Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information\" link and the logic behind it."),
    ("Honor opt-out signals","California and Colorado, among others, require honoring browser signals like Global Privacy Control."),
    ("Minimal forms","Collect only what you need, explain why, and keep sensitive data out of ad platforms."),
    ("A policy that matches reality","The privacy policy must describe what the site actually does. We make sure the site does what your counsel writes.")]},
  {"id":"health","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"Special cases",
   "h2":"Health data<br>and <span class=\"grad\">ad pixels</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Advertising pixels on pages where people book medical appointments or describe health conditions have triggered regulatory warnings and lawsuits. Healthcare providers, wellness businesses and pharmacies should keep tracking off those pages.",
    "HIPAA applies to covered entities and their vendors; state consumer health data laws reach much further. If your site or app touches health, fitness or wellness information, design data collection with counsel from the start.",
    "Children's data is another special case: COPPA applies to sites directed at children under 13 or that knowingly collect their data."]}],
 [("Does the CCPA apply to my small business website?","Only if you meet one of its thresholds — roughly $25 million in revenue (inflation-adjusted), or large volumes of California residents' data, or significant revenue from selling or sharing data. Counsel can confirm."),
  ("Do I need a cookie banner in the US?","Not everywhere, but laws in several states require consent for some processing and opt-outs for targeted advertising. A properly configured banner is the simplest way to cover them."),
  ("What is Global Privacy Control?","A browser signal that tells sites the user opts out of sale or sharing of their data. California and Colorado require businesses covered by their laws to honor it."),
  ("Can you make our site compliant?","We implement the technical side — tracker audit, working consent, opt-out links, GPC support, minimal forms. Your counsel decides the policy and legal obligations.")],
 extra=[("ADA website compliance","/en/ada-website-compliance/")]))

PAGINE.append(guida("/en/outsourcing-software-development-to-europe/",
 "Outsourcing Software Development to Europe",
 "Onshore, offshore or Europe? An honest comparison for US companies outsourcing software: cost, time zones, quality, contracts, IP and when each option fits.",
 "Outsourcing guide",
 "Outsourcing software<br>development <span class=\"grad\">to Europe.</span>",
 "US companies outsourcing software usually compare onshore agencies with offshore teams. Europe is a third option that is less discussed. Here is an honest comparison — including the cases where Europe, and we, are the wrong choice.",
 [{"id":"compare","tipo":"cards","eyebrow":"Three options",
   "h2":"Onshore, offshore<br><span class=\"grad\">and Europe</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Onshore US agency","Same time zone, local presence, highest rates. Worth it when you need people in the room or a large team fast."),
    ("Offshore team","Lowest hourly rates, large time difference with the US, and often higher coordination cost. Works best with very detailed specifications and a strong internal product owner."),
    ("European studio","Rates well below big-city US agencies, a daily morning overlap with US time zones, strong engineering education, and EU-grade privacy habits."),
    ("Freelancers","Flexible and affordable for small, well-defined tasks; a risk for business-critical systems that need continuity."),
    ("Nearshore Latin America","Close time zones and growing talent pools — a good option for teams that need full-day overlap."),
    ("In-house hire","Best for a core product long term; expensive and slow for a single project or internal tool.")]},
  {"id":"tz","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"Time zones",
   "h2":"The overlap<br><span class=\"grad\">that matters</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "From Italy, we are six hours ahead of the East Coast and nine ahead of the West Coast. That gives a daily window in your morning for calls and decisions, and a build that happens while you are offline.",
    "For well-scoped projects, it works well: you send feedback in the evening and review a new version in the morning. For a team that needs to pair with your engineers all afternoon, it does not, and a closer option fits better.",
    "Compared with teams ten or twelve hours away, the difference is that questions get answered the same day rather than the next."]},
  {"id":"contracts","tipo":"testo","eyebrow":"Contracts and IP",
   "h2":"Contracts, IP<br>and <span class=\"grad\">invoices</span>",
   "corpo":[
    "Work with a European supplier is straightforward: contracts in English, intellectual property assigned to your company, code in your repository from the first commit.",
    "Our invoices to US businesses carry no Italian VAT, and payment is by international wire or card in euros. Projects are split into stages, each paid when delivered.",
    "Data protection habits from the GDPR carry over well to US state privacy laws: minimal data collection, documented processing, encryption by default."]},
  {"id":"choose","tipo":"passi","eyebrow":"How to choose",
   "h2":"Four questions<br><span class=\"grad\">to decide</span>",
   "corpo":[
    ("Do you need people in the room?","If yes, go onshore."),
    ("How much overlap do you need?","Full day: onshore or Latin America. A daily morning window: Europe works. Almost none: offshore can work with strong specifications."),
    ("Who owns product decisions?","Without a strong internal product owner, pick a partner that does analysis and design, not just coding."),
    ("Can you stop after a stage?","Insist on fixed-price stages that each deliver something usable. It is the best protection against any supplier.")]}],
 [("Is outsourcing to Europe cheaper than a US agency?","Usually, yes — often a fraction of a big-city US agency's quote — because salaries and overhead are lower, not because less work goes in."),
  ("Is the code quality different?","Quality depends on the team, not the continent. Ask for code samples, references and code in your repository from day one."),
  ("How do we protect our IP?","Through the contract: IP assignment to your company, NDA where needed, and the code living in your own repository."),
  ("Do you work in English?","Yes, throughout: calls, documentation, code comments and contracts.")],
 extra=[("Software development company USA","/en/software-development-company-usa/")]))

